# Slice 295 probe (VAR-OWN-NESTED-AND-REFERENCE; the book :437, :441, :451): an own field declared inside a `while` body
# of a fn has its one place in the method's var: the statement runs each time it is reached, `m\var\b` reads the place after
# the call.  Entry 7; a wrong value leaves as the exit code (v, then 100 + v).
fn: m () int
    int: i 0
    int: s 0
    while: i < 3
        int: b i * 10
        s: s + b
        i: i + 1
    ---
    return: s
end: m
int: code 7
int: v m()
if: v != 30
    code: v
---
if: code = 7 && m\var\b != 20
    code: 100 + m\var\b
---
sendMessage: exit(exit_code: code; stdout: ""; stderr: "")
return
